\subsection{FooBar Circular References}

\begin{note}
A \lstinline{FooBar} is a class whose attributes (\lstinline{x}, \lstinline{y}, \lstinline{z})
carry no fixed meaning.
Each attribute may hold an integer or another \lstinline{FooBar} instance.
Because the attribute names hint at nothing, careful tracing is required.

Every problem contains two \lstinline{print} statements inside a \lstinline{try} block:
the first from a chain of attribute accesses (for example, \lstinline{t.y.y.x}),
and the second from a call to a recursive method (for example, \lstinline{t.wob()}).
When no exception is raised, both lines are printed.
When an exception is raised, Python exits the \lstinline{try} block immediately and
only the first line (or the name of the exception) is printed.

Some problems build structures with a \emph{circular reference},
either accidentally inside \lstinline{__init__} or on purpose via assignment after construction.
A circular reference does not always cause infinite recursion:
\begin{itemize}
\item an attribute chain (\lstinline{t.y.y.y.y}) terminates after a fixed number of steps regardless of cycles,
\item a recursive method that bounds its depth also terminates,
\item a recursive method that walks the cycle without a bound raises \lstinline{RecursionError},
\item accessing an attribute on an \lstinline{int} or \lstinline{None} raises \lstinline{AttributeError},
\item adding to \lstinline{None} (for example, \lstinline{None + 5}) raises \lstinline{TypeError}.
\end{itemize}
The exceptions \lstinline{RecursionError}, \lstinline{AttributeError}, and \lstinline{TypeError} are caught,
and the name of the exception is printed.
Some problems would raise an exception on the second print, but the first print raises one first,
so only the first exception name is observed.

The first problem is a correct reference implementation.
The remaining problems introduce a bug or a variation.
\end{note}
